          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My HbA1c came back at 7.2%. What does that mean, what target does guidance describe, and which code would type 2 diabetes be recorded under?",
          "answer": "You should discuss your HbA1c result of 7.2% with your healthcare provider to understand any necessary adjustments in your management plan.\n\nAn HbA1c level of 7.2% indicates that your average blood sugar level over the previous two to three months has been above the target range typically recommended for individuals with diabetes. Generally, an HbA1c of less than 7% is considered a common target for most adults with type 2 diabetes, although individual goals may vary based on specific patient factors (source: condition-type-2-diabetes).\n\nThe National Diabetes Association guidelines suggest an HbA1c target of around 7% for many adults with diabetes, while also emphasizing that this target should be individualized based on factors such as age, duration of diabetes, and other health conditions (source: guideline-type-2-diabetes-glycemic-targets). Some guidelines allow for a more personalized range rather than specifying a single target (source: guideline-type-2-diabetes-glycemic-targets).\n\nIn terms of medical coding, type 2 diabetes is classified under the code E11 in the ICD-10-CM system, which includes various aspects of the condition, including associated complications (source: coding-type-2-diabetes-e11).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
